;; Reviewed 2026-10-09 by the user (written by Claude, from design
;; section 14, the LLVM backend's Out and FormattedText, the guest runs
;; and the debugger run). Why this is right:
;; - Out keeps its line in OUT_LINE, 1024 bytes, and its length, a LONGINT,
;;   in OUT_LENGTH. Char writes the line first when it is full, then stores
;;   the character, its index checked (trap 2), and counts it.
;; - Ln (WriteLine) calls POC_PUT_LINE with the line's address and its
;;   length, SHORT'ed to an INTEGER (CVTLW) and passed as a longword
;;   (CVTWL), then clears the length; POC_PUT_LINE writes the bytes as
;;   one record with LIB$PUT_OUTPUT. Open and Flush do nothing.
;; - The initializer clears the length and passes the addresses of the
;;   line and the length to POC_OUT_REGISTER, which declares the exit
;;   handler that writes a line left without Ln (OutUnfinished's run on
;;   the VAX).
;; - String copies its argument at entry, as any value open array, and
;;   calls Char for each character before the first 0X or the end.
;; - Int writes MIN(HUGEINT) as a string (its magnitude does not fit);
;;   else it takes the magnitude (MNEGL, MNEGL, SBWC), its digits by
;;   POC_HMOD and POC_HDIV by 10 into the end of digits, the index kept in
;;   R2 across the calls (saved by both masks), a "-" before them for a
;;   negative x, then the blanks that fill the field (Spaces, n less the
;;   digits' count widened to a quadword) and the digits.
;; - Hex takes n into 1..16, then all 16 digits of x's two's complement by
;;   POC_HMOD and POC_HDIV by 16 (floored, so they shift right with the
;;   sign); a non-negative x gets as many as it needs if more than n,
;;   a negative one exactly n, as FormattedText.Hex does.
;; - The debugger run ends with OUT_LINE holding the last line, "the end",
;;   and OUT_LENGTH 0, Ln having written it.
; Out, written by poc (doc/developer/vax-macro32-backend.md)
	.TITLE	OUT
	.IDENT	/2e074f2a93375c1d/
